WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.990
(band stops playing)
(audience cheering)

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.040
- Welcome back y'all.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:05.430
Today we honor the legacy of
Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.740
I am here with Cedric the
Entertainer and Pam Grier,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.350
give it up.
(audience cheering)

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:13.440
My next guest is the drummer
and front man for The Roots.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.630
We love him. You can catch
him five nights a week

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.460
on The Tonight Show with Jimmy Fallon.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.680
Last year, he won an Oscar
(horn noises)

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.677
for his documentary

00:00:20.677 --> 00:00:22.197
"Summer of Soul"
(audience cheering)

00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:24.240
and this year, y'all,
he might do it again.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.640
He is the executive
producer of "Descendant"

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.190
a new Netflix documentary
that traces the legacy

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.666
of the last slave ship to
land in the United States.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:35.100
- [Narrator] What I know of
the story is that it came

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.640
down to a bet and Timothy Mayor
said, "Yeah we can do this".

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.550
And Captain William Foster,
he built "The Clotilda",

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.300
set sail in April of 1860
and went into Dahomey,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.600
at the time, now, Benin,
captured these individuals,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.820
came back into Mobile Bay and was able to

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.360
release these people, sell them to buyers,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.073
and enslaved them.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.900
Foster was able to take it
up the river and burn it.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.855
What was left, eventually
sunk into the river.

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:07.688
(audience clapping)

00:01:07.688 --> 00:01:08.580
- Dialing in from New York,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.835
please welcome Ahmir
Questlove Thompson, everybody.

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:15.480
(audience cheering, band playing)

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.730
What's up, Questlove! Say
hi to Cedric, say hi to Pam.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.009
- Hey, what up, dawg!

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:23.643
- Hey, what's up. How
you doing? Hello, Pam.

00:01:23.643 --> 00:01:26.310
- So Questlove, this story was never told

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.210
in the history books.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.560
I had never heard it,
watched the documentary.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.520
I found it completely

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.170
interesting, appalling,
like, all the things.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.240
There were so many words
to describe it, but it,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.920
these are the stories. I
feel like there's so many

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.570
like this that have not been told.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:41.550
How do you think this happens?

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.700
- Well, you know, there is,
there's a commonality in terms

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.730
of why, you know, education is important.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:50.580
Like, we're currently living
in a time in which we're kind

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:53.910
of debating is like, you
know, is critical race theory

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.680
important to teach at the schools?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.100
Like, this story is a prime
example of why that's important

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.030
because, you know, not only
did you know, but I was well

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.000
into my late 40's before

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:06.990
I knew,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.530
and this is why acknowledging
history is important and why,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.360
you know, erasure is real.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.310
You know, we can't know as a world where

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:16.590
we're going unless we
know where we came from.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.630
- So we don't repeat it.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.670
Well, before the taping
we asked our audience

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.310
to watch the film. It's powerful, right?

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.610
(audience cheering)
Like, I watched it last night

00:02:27.979 --> 00:02:29.520
and honestly there's, so I felt

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.580
like watching the documentary,
literally, there were like,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.470
almost like, four documentaries in one.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.450
Like, you could go off
on different aspects

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:37.530
of this and dive deeper,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.810
'cause there's so many things happening

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.350
in this community and
just overall in society.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.330
So this movie is personal to you, though

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.650
because you're not only a producer

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:48.630
but also a descendant, right?

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.130
- Well, it's personal to
me because back in 2017,

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.790
I was a guest on the PBS
show "Finding Your Roots",

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.290
with Skip Gates. And it was
there, for the first time

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:04.759
in my life, that I had heard of Benin,

00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:07.320
that I heard the name
Charles and Maggie Lewis,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.060
that I heard of an Africatown.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.760
You know, 1% of African
Americans in this country

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:16.680
can name their,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.550
their slave ship, can name the
country that they came from.

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:24.330
Like 99% of us don't know what
part of Africa we came from.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:27.030
That's like one of the largest
continents in the world.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.820
So, you know, that makes
you feel complete inside.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.790
Like I did the show and suddenly, I came

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.340
from not knowing I had
a history to knowing

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.363
that I'm literally somebody.
Like, I know where I came from.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.500
So when the story of this film came up,

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.990
I just wanted to be a
part of it just so that

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.500
I can have life completion and
on knowing who my family is.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.810
But yeah,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:55.893
if any way that I can a elevate
or bring forth this story,

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.810
amplify it, I'm with
it. Absolutely with it.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:01.643
- Yeah.

00:04:01.643 --> 00:04:03.690
So, Questlove's band-mate
Black Thought is also

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.630
an executive producer on the
film, and the movie was picked

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.330
up by Barack and Michelle
Obama's production company.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:12.270
So Questlove, why do you
think these stories are so

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.383
important to share?

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.763
- You know, I think for me personally,

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:19.563
again,

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:23.430
it's a feeling of
completion. You know, again,

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:27.090
like, I have friends who
know that they're Italian.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.310
I have friends that know
that they're from France or

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:33.390
Switzerland or, you know,
and not many black people

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.077
in the United States could say, like,

00:04:35.077 --> 00:04:36.600
"Oh, my family came from Ghana",

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.210
or, "My family came from", you know,

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:42.063
South Africa, or any other
part of the continent.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.033
And as a result there might be a

00:04:46.033 --> 00:04:47.673
a kind of nihilistic,

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:50.610
incompletion,

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:53.070
incomplete feeling that
we have as human beings.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.340
I instantly know that the second I heard,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.340
like, the name Benin and,
you know, where I came from,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.220
instantly my whole
demeanor changed, you know,

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.390
and that's why I feel as
though this is important

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:11.390
because we now have the tools
to see where we came from.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.320
- Yeah.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.380
- So now's I think, now is the first time

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.590
in our lives in which we can
find out what our history is.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:20.940
That's why it's important.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.410
- Yeah. Connection is important.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.330
I feel like, and also I
feel like these stories

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:29.190
are important too, because
it really does show you,

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.860
just slaps you in the face with imbalance.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:34.920
Opportunity is not there for people.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.140
Education to even help
their own community,

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.370
is not available to people.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.380
Like, it's, it really does,

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.360
I think the documentary does a great job

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.580
of depicting imbalance
and how true that is.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.770
People talk about it,
but it does a great job.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:48.630
You visually see it happening

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.810
and you experience it with
the people of this town.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.700
- The way my mother told me this,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.810
that Timothy Mayer, a local businessman,

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.660
made a bet that after
slavery was abolished

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.960
that he could still bring
Africans into the country.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:08.310
And he went and got a bunch
of Africans and brought us,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:12.820
brought them back here to
the mouth of the Mobile River

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.180
just let 'em off the boat

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.093
and burned the ship to conceal the crime.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.750
- Welcome back on this
Martin Luther King Jr. Day.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.658
I'm here with Cedric the
Entertainer and Pam Grier.

00:06:24.658 --> 00:06:26.376
(audience cheering)

00:06:26.376 --> 00:06:28.083
That was a clip from the
new Netflix documentary,

00:06:28.083 --> 00:06:30.810
"Descendant", which
chronicles 'The Clotilda',

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.270
the last slave ship to come to America.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:36.660
It also documents modern day
Africatown, in Mobile, Alabama.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.730
Residents there are said
to be the descendants

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.580
of that ship and that
legacy was proven true when

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.190
they found 'The Clotilda' in 2019.

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:46.890
It's executive-produced by
Ahmir Questlove Thompson

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.380
and he's on the line
with us from New York.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.780
It also features my next
guest. Like Questlove,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.910
she's a direct descendant
from The Clotilda

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:55.860
and she still lives in
the area to this day.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.193
Please welcome Veda Tunstall, y'all.

00:06:58.193 --> 00:07:02.313
(audience cheering)
- Hello.

00:07:03.789 --> 00:07:05.848
I literally am, no B.S.,

00:07:05.848 --> 00:07:07.500
I connected with you so much in the film.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.440
I feel like we have
similar thoughts and when

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.690
you're talking about things on, you know,

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:17.460
justice and "how do you
fix it, where do you go?"

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.580
And, and even leaving things
open to like, we need help.

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:21.960
Like, there's, what do you do?

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.940
Like, ideas, and I felt
like I connected with you

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:24.773
so much on that.

00:07:24.773 --> 00:07:27.630
So, "Descendant" connects
the past to the present.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.150
So, tell us about the
challenges facing your community

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.460
'cause like, even how it
leaves off the documentary,

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.770
like, what it leaves off
with like, it's kind of like,

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.900
open-ended like, things are
still trying to progress.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.520
So what, where are you at right now?

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.610
- There are organizations
and people who've been

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.200
at work for all these years.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.050
Like, it's not new that work
is being done in Africatown

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:47.640
but there's still a
lot of work to be done.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.650
It's still true that the
community is surrounded

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:51.930
on all sides by heavy industry.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.780
There's work being done to
mitigate the negative impact

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.613
of those,

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:00.180
those plants and all
the chemical, you know,

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.610
- The amount of people
with cancer is insane.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:03.443
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:08:03.443 --> 00:08:05.460
- And I was just talking to
a friend of mine a few days

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.680
ago who used to teach at
Mobile County Training School

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.350
and she said somebody needs
to do some research on this

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.650
because the number of children
with neurological disorders

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.140
and intellectual
disabilities, she was like,

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.510
it's gotta be because the
soil is toxic, the air

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:20.460
is toxic and the water is toxic.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.300
So, today there's a lot
of work being done on the,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.837
with Environmental Justice or I call it

00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:28.880
"environmental injustice", that we are,

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.620
we were just in Washington
DC, speaking with people

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:36.180
from the EPA about resources
to help Africatown with that.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.480
This community was self-sustaining
some years ago until

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.190
it was incorporated
into the city of Mobile.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.213
So there were,

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.540
there were businesses,
there was healthcare

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:48.110
there was everything there,
but now it's not there, so...

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:50.100
- They addressed that in the documentary.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:51.300
He was like, "I used to live here.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.070
I used to live across the
street." Now it's all changed

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:54.150
- It's all gone.
- Yeah.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.070
- So now that this film has come out

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.580
it's shined a light over the
whole world on Africatown.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.720
So what we are hoping now
is that because everybody's

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.300
paying attention that we can do something

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.293
about these things.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:13.130
We are already speaking with
the EPA and federal government,

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:16.140
local government, you know,
we are, we're having to work

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:20.280
with our local government
because they're not really

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.070
addressing the issues
like we need them to.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.843
So we are still working on that.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.020
We need access to healthcare.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.030
There's no clinic in
Africatown, people have

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:31.830
transportation issues.

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:35.080
So, even though there are needs
in the community, there is a

00:09:36.278 --> 00:09:37.710
it's a proud community.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:41.010
People are working hard
to bring Africatown back

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:43.620
to the community that it
used to be, self-sustaining.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.570
- Flourish, like it was. Yeah.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:48.210
I think, that is also
what's so important about

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:49.710
and you know, thank you Quest. Thank you,

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:51.960
all these producers that
are, you're shining a light.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.010
You're saying, "Well we've been doing this

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.480
for a minute," but see, nobody knew

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.180
like, this is important
that people shine a light.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.670
People that have a spotlight
to take that spotlight

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:01.320
shine it where it needs to go.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:02.427
And really, 'cause there are
so many people that watch that

00:10:02.427 --> 00:10:06.960
go, "What can I do? What
can I do? How can I help?"

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.707
You know, there are people
watching that are like,

00:10:08.707 --> 00:10:11.250
"Wait, I know I've worked with,
you know, the EPA on this.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:12.480
Maybe I can help here at this".

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.020
People, I think I have to
believe always that there's hope

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.700
like that, that people want to help.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.470
And I just think this
is what's so important

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:21.120
about films like this and
documentaries about this

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:24.032
is that this is real
life. This isn't a movie.

00:10:24.032 --> 00:10:25.043
- Right. Right.

00:10:25.043 --> 00:10:27.134
- This is, these are
people's lives, you know?

00:10:27.134 --> 00:10:30.231
And to call it like what
it is, to say what it is,

00:10:30.231 --> 00:10:32.370
you know, gentrification,
to say what it is.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:33.870
These people are encroaching on it.

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.190
They want everybody to leave.

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.660
They wanna keep their
plants there, they don't

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:37.493
wanna change it.

00:10:37.493 --> 00:10:39.600
That's 'cause it's greed
and power rule the world.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.433
- Absolutely.

00:10:40.433 --> 00:10:42.660
And you know, at the end
it's not, nothing's changed.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.370
People need help. Like, people need that.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:47.250
So that's why I'm so glad
that you're here talking to us

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.083
about it.

00:10:48.083 --> 00:10:51.030
And I'm so glad that
people watching will get to

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.590
maybe have that impact.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:54.900
Well, we reached out to
our friends at My Heritage,

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.480
which lets anyone easily
trace their family history

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.700
through their website, myheritage.com

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:02.790
They love the work The
Africatown Heritage Preservation

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:04.710
Foundation is doing for the community.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.930
So they're gonna donate $10,000 to help

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:08.771
out 'cause they were touched by y'all.

00:11:08.771 --> 00:11:11.880
(audience cheering)

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.090
As we mentioned, "Descendant" is released

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.640
through The Obama's production company.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.500
Michelle Obama is encouraging everyone to

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.290
share their family history
through photos and stories.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.140
Use the hashtag #descendantchallenge.

